      "role": "user",
      "content": "The diagram below shows three different substances, P, Q and R. KEY: Hydrogen, Chlorine, Oxygen, Sulphur, Sodium, Iron.\n\nQuestion: Define the term element."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is stuck, so I need a simple, low‑cognitive‑load prompt that nudges them toward the core idea of an element without giving the definition away. Asking about the word “pure” or about a single‑atom example will reveal whether they recognise the ‘one‑type‑of‑atom’ concept.",
      "content": "Can you think of an example of a substance that is made up of only one kind of atom? (Just give any example you know, even if you’re not sure.)"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Um… maybe hydrogen? I think it’s only hydrogen atoms, but I’m not really sure."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner mentioned hydrogen, which is indeed an element consisting of only hydrogen atoms. This shows they are on the right track. I will now confirm the correct definition, keep the response short, and add the required formula breakdown and exam tip.",
      "content": "Yes, hydrogen is a good example – it is made up of only hydrogen atoms.\n\n**Final Answer:** A pure substance consisting of one type of atom.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\[ \\text{Element} = \\text{one type of atom} \\]\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(\\text{Element}\\): the substance we are defining.\n  * \\(\\text{one type of atom}\\): all the particles in the substance are identical atoms (e.g., all hydrogen atoms).\n* **Why we use it:** This description tells us that an element cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical means because it already consists of a single kind of atom.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In the exam, the definition is worth 1 mark. Make sure you include the words *pure* and *one type of atom* – omitting either word can lose the mark.\n"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
